winex11: Offered the focus for a window a modal dialog disabled, activate the dialog.

On Windows, clicking a window that a modal dialog has disabled activates
the dialog: the last active popup of the top of the window's owner chain.
Under X the window manager raises the window it offers the focus to and
sends WM_TAKE_FOCUS. Since 0022 Wine takes the focus for a disabled window
(an owner disabled for an out-of-process dialog must not end up
unreachable), and with an offer it considers stale it does nothing at all;
either way the dialog stays below, or loses the focus. In Affinity the
recovery prompt went behind the startup dialog when that was clicked or
alt-tabbed to.

When the offered window is disabled and the top of its owner chain has an
enabled, visible last active popup other than itself, focus that popup and
ask the window manager to activate it, which raises it. GA_ROOTOWNER is not
used: it stops at a window without WS_POPUP, as these dialogs are. An owner
disabled for the portal file chooser has no such popup and keeps 0022's
behavior.

The request must not be skipped as a duplicate. The dialog's own
activation request, made when it was shown, can still look pending: KWin
had already made it active, so _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW never changed to confirm
it. KWin may also send the property change for the window it is activating
only after WM_TAKE_FOCUS, so nothing has cleared that state yet, and
set_net_active_window returned without sending anything. KWin kept the
disabled window active, and Wine then followed it.

In a test with an owner, an enabled palette, a disabled owned dialog and a
modal prompt, under a nested KWin, the prompt lost the activation to the
disabled dialog when KWin activated that; with this it stays active and on
top, repeatedly, and when KWin activates the owner.

diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
index 226adc7..2817c03 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
@@ -603,6 +603,41 @@ static void handle_wm_protocols( HWND hwnd, XClientMessageEvent *event )
     {
         HWND last_focus = x11drv_thread_data()->last_focus, foreground = NtUserGetForegroundWindow();
 
+        /* On Windows, clicking a window that a modal dialog has disabled
+         * activates the dialog instead: the last active popup of the top of
+         * the window's owner chain. The window manager raises whatever it
+         * offers the focus to, so taking the focus for the disabled window, or
+         * ignoring the offer as stale, left the dialog behind it; activate the
+         * dialog and ask the window manager to raise it. */
+        if (!window_has_pending_wm_state( hwnd, -1 ) && !NtUserIsWindowEnabled( hwnd ))
+        {
+            HWND owner = hwnd, next, popup = 0;
+
+            /* the top of the owner chain; GA_ROOTOWNER stops at a window that
+             * is not WS_POPUP, which these dialogs usually are not */
+            while ((next = NtUserGetWindowRelative( owner, GW_OWNER ))) owner = next;
+
+            /* GetLastActivePopup, as Windows uses: an enabled owned palette is
+             * not the dialog, and an owner disabled for an out-of-process
+             * dialog (the portal file chooser) has no popup of its own here,
+             * so it keeps the focus as before. */
+            if (owner) popup = NtUserGetLastActivePopup( owner );
+            if (popup && popup != owner && popup != hwnd && NtUserIsWindowEnabled( popup ) &&
+                NtUserIsWindowVisible( popup ) && can_activate_window( popup, FALSE ))
+            {
+                TRACE( "window %p is disabled, activating its enabled popup %p\n", hwnd, popup );
+                set_focus( event->display, popup, event_time );
+                /* The window manager has made hwnd active, and may send the
+                 * _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW change only after this message. A request
+                 * for popup can still look pending -- the window manager had
+                 * granted it already, so the property never changed to confirm
+                 * it -- and set_net_active_window would then skip this one. */
+                x11drv_thread_data()->pending_state.net_active_window = None;
+                set_net_active_window( popup, hwnd );
+                return;
+            }
+        }
+
         if (window_has_pending_wm_state( hwnd, -1 ) || (hwnd != foreground && !window_should_take_focus( foreground, event_time )))
         {
             WARN( "Ignoring window %p/%lx WM_TAKE_FOCUS serial %lu, event_time %ld, foreground %p during WM_STATE change\n",
